    overview: "The sixth observance is not to stop at avoiding the forbidden, but to keep away from the doubtful too. The trader should not look only to the legal rulings but consult his own heart. He should ask where goods come from when they seem suspect, and refuse to deal with oppressors and their helpers, even to build a wall for the Muslims.",
    thesis: "A trader guarding his religion goes beyond the rulings. If his heart feels unease, he avoids the thing. Dealing with oppressors is the strongest kind of help to them, and the early Muslims would not hand one an inkpot without knowing what he would write.",
    moves: [
      { title: "Sixth: beyond the forbidden", body: "The sixth observance is not to stop at avoiding what is forbidden, but to guard against the places of doubt and the occasions of suspicion. He should not look to the legal rulings, but ask his own heart. If he finds unease in it, he should avoid the thing. When goods are brought to him and something about them troubles him, he should ask about them until he knows. Otherwise he will eat what is doubtful." },
      { title: "Where is this from?", body: "Milk was brought to the messenger of God. He asked: “Where did you get this?” They said: “From the sheep.” He asked: “And where did you get the sheep?” He was told: “From such a place.” Then he drank, and said: “We prophets are commanded to eat only what is good and to do only what is right.” He said: “God has commanded the believers what He commanded the messengers,” and recited: “You who believe, eat of the good things We have provided you.” But the Prophet did not ask about everything brought to him." },
      { title: "Whom to refuse", body: "What is required is that the trader looks at whom he deals with. He should not deal with anyone known for wrongdoing, dishonesty, theft or usury. He should never deal with soldiers and oppressors, or with their friends and helpers, because by doing so he helps oppression." },
      { title: "The wall on the frontier", body: "A man took on the job of building a wall for a fortress on the frontier. He said: “Something troubled me about it. The work was good, even one of the duties of Islam. But the governor in charge of that area was an oppressor. I asked Sufyan, and he said: do not help them in anything, small or great. I said: this is a wall in God's cause, for the Muslims. He said: yes. But the least that will happen to you is that you will want them to stay in power, so that they pay your wage. Then you will have loved the survival of someone who disobeys God.”" },
      { title: "Praying for an oppressor", body: "A report says: “Whoever prays for an oppressor to live long has loved that God be disobeyed on His earth.” Another says: “God is angered when a wicked man is praised.” Another says: “Whoever honours a wicked man has helped to tear down Islam.”" },
      { title: "The inkpot and the clay", body: "Sufyan went in to the caliph al-Mahdi, who had a white scroll in his hand. Al-Mahdi said: “Sufyan, pass me the inkpot so I can write.” Sufyan said: “Tell me what you will write. If it is right, I will give it to you.” A ruler asked a scholar he had imprisoned to hand him some clay to seal a letter. The scholar said: “Hand me the letter first, so I can see what is in it.” This is how carefully they avoided helping oppressors. Dealing with them is the strongest kind of help, and people of religion should avoid it as far as they can." },
      { title: "Fewer to deal with", body: "In short, people should fall, in his eyes, into those he deals with and those he does not. In this age, those he deals with should be fewer than those he does not. Someone said: “There was a time when a man would come to the market and ask: whom do you advise me to deal with? He was told: deal with anyone you like. Then came a time when they said: deal with anyone except so-and-so and so-and-so. Then came another time when it was said: deal with no one except so-and-so and so-and-so. I fear a time will come when that too is gone.” Ghazali adds that what he feared seems to have come about. We belong to God, and to Him we return." },
    ],
